[M30FinalContract {
    ProtocolModel: "Dedicated transfer queue means a transfer-capable queue family that is independent enough to overlap staging copies with compute work. Transfer queue executes host->device uploads for A/B (and optionally device->host readback for C). Compute queue executes SGEMM dispatch and any compute-local barriers. Synchronization edges are host-write->transfer-read, transfer-write->compute-read, compute-write->readback-transfer-read, readback-transfer-write->host-read, plus explicit queue-family ownership handoff when families differ. Fixed-double slots keep deterministic current/next ownership with generation guards and in-flight transfer/compute rejection."
    QueueConfigurations: "Model includes: (1) no dedicated transfer queue fallback to single queue; (2) real dedicated transfer queue with explicit semaphores and ownership transfer; (3) pseudo-transfer/shared-family configuration that must not overclaim overlap unless policy gates prove benefit."
    FixedDoubleInteraction: "Slot lifecycle states are empty, preparing transfer, transfer in-flight, transfer complete, ready for compute, compute in-flight, consumed, failed. Each slot tracks slot id, generation, shape/layout metadata, queue ownership, ready flag, transfer in-flight, compute in-flight, and failure state."
    MandatorySynchronizationEdges: "Mandatory edges: host write visible before transfer read; transfer completion + visibility before compute read; compute completion + visibility before readback transfer read; readback completion + visibility before host read; and all queue completions before async ready is reported. Missing edges are protocol hazards and must be rejected."
    MandatoryOwnershipRules: "If queue families differ, ownership release/acquire is mandatory at transfer->compute and compute->transfer/readback transitions. Slot reuse is forbidden while transfer or compute is in-flight. Failure is generation-scoped so transfer failure in next slot cannot poison current compute slot."
    PolicyRecommendation: "Use separate policies: upload-only (lower risk) and upload+readback (higher sync complexity). Enable dedicated transfer queue only under fallback gating: dedicated queue exists, independence indicates useful overlap, expected overlap exceeds sync cost, and slot ownership/sync graph is valid."
    DiagnosticsContract: "Native diagnostics must expose transfer queue used/not used, selected policy, dedicated availability, fallback reason, ownership handoff count, transfer/compute wait count, transfer failure slot, compute failure slot, async readiness state, slot id + generation, and upload-only vs upload+readback mode."
    NotModeled: "M30 intentionally does not model Vulkan API objects, exact barrier flags, command buffer lifetime internals, allocator internals, hardware timings, triple buffering, or async architecture redesign."
    FinalRecommendation: "Dedicated transfer queue is worth implementing only when truly independent and gated; the main value is fixed-double overlap of slot N compute with slot N+1 upload. Default to single queue on no-dedicated, pseudo-transfer, or low-benefit small-shape regimes."
    M31Direction: "M31 should implement native upload-only dedicated transfer queue first with explicit semaphore + ownership handoff instrumentation, preserve single-queue fallback, and add conformance tests for all mandatory synchronization/ownership diagnostics before considering upload+readback."
}]
